A||||4||rodmcneill||Speaker and cover change||||1||
Z||000000||rodmcneill||03-06-2001||12:26 AM||||I just got done installing some Pioneer 4x10s that I had bought used off Ebay. I had not realised how bad my old speakers had gotten. Wow! what a difference, much better highs and I now have bass! At higher levels I can see the speaker covers flex! The original covers do seem to block a fair amount of the sound however so I am thinking about integrating the Pioneer open grill into the original cloth grill. I will probably try to pick up some used tan grills to experiment on. I was thinking about a 1" border left around the original grill, some of the perimeter of the wire mesh grill removed and fastened-glued together. I think the cloth grills look nice but a little dated, wire mesh would jazz up the dash a little and you would not have to worry about fading and tears as much.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||03-06-2001||12:58 AM||||What I did was removed the center out of the factory grill covers then I covered the entire thing with grill cloth from Radio Shack. They have black and brown. If you look from the bottom side you'll notice it's plastic with many holes. I removed all the plastic holed pieces and ripped off the old cloth. If you stretch the new grill cloth over it tightly it won't "vibrate" and your mids and highs will sound even better. To attach the new grill cloth I used 5-minute epoxy on one side. Let it dry for a few hours to cure very well, then stretch the new cloth across tightly and epoxy that side. I used some pins to hold it in place while the epoxy dried then I did the ends.||||reg||1
Z||000002||deceler8||03-06-2001||09:33 AM||||Another option is to tear off the cloth altogether. <P>If you have a gray dash, the uncovered grill actually looks pretty good. And if you don't, the grills are plastic and easily painted to match.||||reg||1
Z||000003||286SE'S||03-06-2001||02:45 PM||||I picked up a pair of speaker covers at the junkyard and removed the plastic grillwork under the cloth thinking I would get so much more out of the tweeter and midrange. Well, it made absolutely no difference at all. Now I have saggy cloth and nasty looking speakers. Where did I put those old ones?||||reg||1
Z||000004||overlord||03-06-2001||07:45 PM||||the only reasons i would take apart my speaker grills is becaause my aftermarket speakers touch the grill. Damn tweeters are a tad too tall.  Plus i have 1 little hole in each grill, probably from some idiot trying to figure out the best way to stick a screwdriver in the grill to pop it out.||||reg||1
